{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1385 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "957c122bd6bbdb853eeaf138769ec32d7bc3d46aaeff1abcf720b217709114f5" :edb/db-before-t 1384 :edb/db-after-t 1385 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534697 50 #inst "2026-10-12T08:12:55.348Z" 13194139534697 17592186045417 true] [17592186054833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433174 13194139534697 17592186045417 true] [17592186119925 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433173 13194139534697 17592186045417 true] [17592186119944 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433175 13194139534697 17592186045417 true] [17592186305140 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+12{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta +12\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-12\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha +12\\beta =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha +12\\beta =11\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{19}{24},\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{19}{24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+(-\\frac{19}{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(\\frac{1}{8})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$" 13194139534697 17592186045417 true] [17592186305140 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n4\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+12{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(12\\beta +12\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-12\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha +12\\beta =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -12\\alpha +12\\beta =11\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{19}{24},\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{19}{24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+(-\\frac{19}{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(\\frac{1}{8})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$" 13194139534697 17592186045417 false] [17592186433154 1043 #edb/ref 17592186433176 13194139534697 17592186045417 true] [17592186433154 1043 #edb/ref 17592186433177 13194139534697 17592186045417 true] [17592186433154 1043 #edb/ref 17592186433178 13194139534697 17592186045417 true] [17592186433154 1043 #edb/ref 17592186433179 13194139534697 17592186045417 true] [17592186433154 1043 #edb/ref 17592186433180 13194139534697 17592186045417 true] [17592186433154 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045460 13194139534697 17592186045417 true] [17592186433154 1045 "Quiz 6" 13194139534697 17592186045417 true] [17592186433154 1046 #edb/ref 17592186433177 13194139534697 17592186